  "result": "All three tests pass.\n\n**Approach chosen:** Approach A, adjacency sets. Every method in the fixed interface is neighbour-oriented (sorted neighbour lists, BFS over neighbours, an edge counter), so a per-node set gives O(1) edge lookup and O(degree) iteration without the n\u00b2 memory an adjacency matrix would need, which matters most for the sparse graphs the tests exercise.\n\nImplementation is in `netgraph/graph.py`:\n- Edges are stored in both endpoint sets, with a self-loop stored once as a node being its own neighbour, so the edge counter increments once per distinct edge.\n- Out-of-range ids raise IndexError via a shared bounds check on every public method that takes a node.\n- Shortest path is a BFS that records parents and reconstructs the path when the target is first discovered, returning `[u]` for `u == v` and None when unreachable.\n\n```\npython -m pytest -q\n3 passed in 0.02s\n```",
